WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.420
Hey guys, it's Jan here.

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:05.990
As you know, I really enjoyed the Deadpool
movie and I'm psyched to hear that a sequel

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:09.050
has already been greenlit and is being written
by the same screenwriters.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:14.070
In this video, I'm going to breakdown Deadpool's
post-credits scenes and talking what it means

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.070
for the sequel.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:18.700
There will be spoilers ahead, so if you're
not ready for that, check out my non-spoiler

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.980
review of the movie here!

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.230
Deadpool's post-credits is a perfect mix of
tribute to John Hughes's classic 80s comedy

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:30.279
Ferris Bueller's Day Off and sly dig at Marvel's
post-credits scenes, which typically tease

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:34.449
future instalments in the Marvel Cinematic
Universe and have featured Samuel L Jackson

00:00:34.449 --> 00:00:40.230
as the leather-clad, eye-patch-wearing SHIELD
director Nick Fury at end of Iron Man, Thor,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.409
and Captain America: The First Avenger.

00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:46.579
Deadpool's joke that 'Oh, you're expecting
a teaser for Deadpool 2; well, we don't have

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:51.460
that kind of money', again is a crack at not
only Marvel's movies but also other big-budget

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.489
PG-13 superhero movies such as the X-Men franchise.

00:00:55.489 --> 00:00:59.519
And the influence of Ferris Bueller is obvious
in the look of Deadpool's post-credits in

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:04.100
which our anti-hero dons a Bueller-style dressing
gown, and appears in a room which owes a lot

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:08.340
to the classic comedy thanks to the positioning
of its two doors, the pictures on the wall,

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.390
and its flowery wallpaper.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:14.140
And it's also clear in Deadpool's fourth-wall-breaking
line: 'You're still here!

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.680
It's over, go home.'

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.780
And later he also tells us to 'Go!', all of
which mimics Ferris Bueller's words, as does

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:23.990
the way Deadpool says them while emerging
from the door and walking towards the camera.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:28.360
And when Deadpool says 'chika-chika' at the
very end he's imitating the same sound which

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:33.009
appears at the end of Ferris Bueller's post-credits
that's taken from Yello's song 'Oh Yeah'.

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:37.130
Ok, so, in the second part of Deadpool's post-credits
we get the reveal that there's going to be

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.039
a sequel featuring the comic book character
Cable.

00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:44.429
Deadpool playfully mentions that they're looking
to cast someone, 'a big guy with a flat top,

00:01:44.429 --> 00:01:47.499
like Mel Gibson, Dolph Lundgren, or Keira
Knightley'

00:01:47.499 --> 00:01:52.479
It's interesting Deadpool mentions Mel Gibson
because the cover of Deadpool #41 is a fantastic

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:57.119
homage to the original Mad Max trilogy in
which Mel Gibson of course played Max.

00:01:57.119 --> 00:02:02.229
And the score to the Deadpool movie is by
Junkie XL who also scored Mad Max: Fury Road.

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:06.909
As for Dolph Lundgren, he played The Punisher
in the 1989 movie and The Punisher has appeared

00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:09.050
in the Deadpool: Suicide Kings comics.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.060
The Keira Knightley reference, as well as
being a funny joke about her acting range,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.840
could also be a crafty call-back to the fact
she played bounty hunter Domino Harvey in

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:22.120
Tony Scott's 2005 action movie Domino, and
in the Marvel comics there's a female mutant

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.620
called Domino who teams up with Cable's mercenary
group Six Pack.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.350
By the way, Deadpool's words, 'Big secret,
shh!', reminded me of the character's appearance

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:34.570
in the post-credits of X-Men Origins: Wolverine,
where we see his severed head breaking the

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:39.550
fourth wall whispering 'shh' to the audience
because at the time no one knows he's still

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:40.550
alive.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:43.660
There've been rumours and support from fans
for a good while now about having Cable in

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:44.660
the sequel.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.880
Cable's the son of Cyclops and Jean Grey-clone
Madelyne Prior.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:52.980
He's a time-travelling mutant cyborg with
a cybernetic eye, bionic arms, superhuman

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.870
strength, and telepathic and telekinetic powers.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:59.730
When Deadpool and Cable first meet they're
enemies, but later they team up and even have

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:01.560
their own comic book series together.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.570
In the comics, Cable also established the
X-Force, basically an off-shoot of the X-Men,

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:10.960
so making a sequel with him might be a good
way for Fox to finally get an X-Force movie

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:11.960
off the ground.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.880
Ryan Reynolds has already said that an X-Force
movie is a priority for him.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:20.550
And Reynolds also posted a teaser video of
him and Olivia Munn who plays Psylocke in

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:27.190
X-Men: Apocalypse, so is he hinting at a potential
Deadpool-Psylocke buddy-up in X-Force?

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:31.330
Going back to character arcs that were left
open in Deadpool, for me I'd love to see Vanessa

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:34.420
get to play her comic book alter ego Copycat.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.770
In the final fight, Vanessa was put in a glass
case like the one Wade was in at the Workshop

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:43.350
and Ajax started suffocating her just as he'd
done to trigger Wade's mutant powers.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:49.090
As Ajax told Wade, stress activates mutant
genes, so perhaps the stress of falling off

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:52.850
an exploding helicarrier in the glass tank
could have triggered some powers in Vanessa

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:54.580
that we'll see in the next movie.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:58.680
In the comics, her metamorphic powers are
so strong that she can replicate another mutant's

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.640
abilities and their appearance, so she could
be a really interesting character to have

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.230
in the X-Men movie universe.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:07.660
And do you remember Cunningham, the other
patient who Wade talked to at Ajax's workshop?

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:12.490
Well, it's interesting that the film didn't
explicitly seem to kill him off as I think

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:16.190
I saw him being injected and cased up by Angel
Dust later in the movie.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:20.331
Perhaps Ajax was selling him as a super-soldier
or mutant to someone and we'll see his character

00:04:20.331 --> 00:04:21.331
come back?

00:04:21.331 --> 00:04:22.380
What do you think?

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.570
And remember that Colossus tells Deadpool
that "we'll make an X-Man of you yet", so

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:31.420
perhaps we'll see Deadpool pop up in a future
X-Men movie, or maybe a Gambit or Wolverine

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:32.420
movie?

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:37.360
Now, Garrison Kane was in the original screenplay
for Deadpool but ended up getting cut due

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:38.860
to the movie's tight budget.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:43.550
Garrison Kane has huge bionic arms and the
filmmakers thought the additional visual effects

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:45.700
required would be too expensive.

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:50.640
But the character does fit in well with Cable
and X-Force in the comics so it would be interesting

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.400
to see if he makes it into a sequel.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:57.210
By the way, Deadpool director Tim Miller has
said he'd love to see Deadpool fight Wolverine's

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:58.610
female clone X23.

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:02.990
After all, there was that funny moment in
the movie where Deadpool had a momentary moral

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:07.100
quandary about attacking some female goons,
debating out loud whether it was more sexist

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:11.440
not to hit them than to actually hit them,
before he got bored and pulled out his gun.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:15.860
Pitting Deadpool against a female Wolverine
would also get around the fact that Hugh Jackman

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:18.480
has said he wants the next Wolverine movie
to be his last.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.950
So guys what did you think of the post-credits
tease and who would you like to play Cable

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.760
in Deadpool 2?

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:28.600
And are there any other X-Men or characters
you'd like to see in the sequel?

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.070
I can't wait to read your comments below!

